  PowerPoint here after we survive that  and just finish up a bit so some other  things that are going to be happening  near future so in one of our previous  webinars I believe it was the sep tember  webinar we talked about a new ID type  parameter for the e-utilities that is  almost ready now and should be going out  in January and so that will allow the  e-utilities to accept accession versions  as their IDs and allow them to output  those you want more details about that  please refer back to the sep tember 7th  webinar where we discussed that in some  detail and we'll be having much more  publicity about that as as this goes  forward and just also keep in mind this  March date when GIS will no longer be  available in any of our flat file or  fast day presentations that includes all  of the things on the ftp site such as  the genbank and the refseq downloads  alright so I'm going to pause here thank  you everyone for your attention and if  there are questions please let us know  okay are we haven't us several questions  here one question is what about the  other databases that link to ncbi while  they continue to have GIS I not sure I  understand that question completely we  have no control about what happens to  the outside databases so if you want to  clarify that question please type it  into the question spot well second  question is which non database method is  appropriate for say 50 to 500 g I  numbers so if you have a small number 50  to 500 either way works you can  certainly use one effects URL to do that  number and that should be fine and that  means you don't have to download a  database and use this space for it so  I'd probably say you see fetch if you  have a small number like that the script  and database is really designed I would  say if you have more than like ten  thousand okay the last question has to  do with blast about the blast being  updated to handle GIS instead of the  sessions that's already the case so the  current version of blast which is 2.5  point 0 plus works with accessions just  keep in mind that the the GI numbers are  still present in the pre-formatted blast  databases so you can continue to use  those as you always have and then the  other question is I'm not clear why GI  number has changed to accession maybe  that question is about why we made this  change would you like to address that so  the if the basic question is like why  this is happening a couple of things we  can say is just in general over the  course of time we've had two different  ways of identifying sequences one being  the GI numbers and one being accession  dot versions in fact both of these  sequence identifiers are redundant with  one another in that they each uniquely  map to a particular sequence record and  most publications and journals require  accession numbers for publication of  sequences and that's more typical way in  the literature of referring to sequence  data another advantage of the accession  version over the GI is as I mentioned  earlier many of our sequence databases  in fact do not have GI numbers so  important resources such as the wgs data  sets and TSA status data set simply do  not have GI numbers at all and so the  only way that we can refer to those  sequences is by accession version so we  are moving towards a point where we can  universally access all of the sequence  data at NCBI any consistent manner and  that be an accession dot version another  follow-up question  think about blasts for making the GI  lists and things like that to limit  database access and blast so you can  pick which sequences you want to use  your blast database there is a CID list  option that can use accession numbers  we're working on making that option one  problem with that is that the index is  slower to run and so we're working on  making that faster I'm not sure whether  that will be out the next release or not  but you can currently do them with  accessions but they won't be as fast as  the GI numbers of present it's a couple  more questions it would be helpful to  know what the most popular letter  prefixes on the accession dot version  IDs refer to so some of that stuff is on  our ftp buzz on our website if you for  the refseq accession xin particular for  the insd the submitted accession numbers  that's a little bit more complex and  sometimes they're just purely arbitrary  the last question I see and probably the  last one that will take is if I submit a  new sequence it will have a new  accession or version update of an older  sequence so the answer to that question  is if you submit a new sequence it will  get a brand-new accession number and it  will be version 1 so we're continuing to  assign accession numbers because that  space is huge we can continue to do that  essentially forever okay I think that's  all we have time for today if you have  another question that's in the chat pod  that we didn't get to will answer that  in a document I'll turn it back over to  Eric now all right thank you Peter so  again thank you for your attention  hopefully this will be a useful utility  for you give it a try see what happens  let us know if you have any any  questions about that or problems with  that you can write to info INF 0 at ncbi  nlm nih gov I've put some other  additional points information up here  you can follow our blog which is NCBI  insights of the NCBI news and other  social media our Facebook and Twitter  channels for upcoming announcements  about GI and accession number issues  when these things are going to be  released when other changes may be  happening I will be continuing to do  additional webinars on this topic and  again if you are particularly interested  in the api's utilities hyphen announced  is an email listserv where we  announced that's changes and updates for  that community so you may want to sign  up for that and there's a link on this  slide lets you do that and again I'll  certainly you can write to info if you  have additional 